// The call under test, with whatever it needs wrapped around it: output
// captured for a prints/eprints clause, recording turned on for a calls
// clause (pre.id, post.id) -- or, for a case with none of these, exactly the
// call, unchanged.

// KNOWN GAP: this function's own inline cases are not written. It is a thin,
// 3-statement wrapper -- pre, the call, post -- and each of the three is
// cased on its own (pre/pre.id, case/more/call.id (pre-existing), post/
// post.id); what is missing is a fixture giving it casefn/casenode state
// (pre/fixture.id's wrap_fixture) that does not crash the harness once
// emit_case_call_wrapped is the function under test -- pre.id's and post.id's
// own cases, built the same way, run fine. Every other new function in this
// tree has cases; this is the one left, with the investigation above it.
emit_case_call_wrapped(int ci) {
  emit_capture_pre(ci);
  emit_case_call(ci);
  emit_capture_post(ci);
} return void;
